#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "5831f44c-02c6-5a5b-a9e7-58c7ac36cb6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6112" :question/problem "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049092}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517241 :question-generator/id #uuid "e5d9c8a9-e502-52af-b588-07dab00deac0" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186064271.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84db6e6b-7217-5d54-9e58-abd0eb0b10b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollar per ton squared" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063014} {:answer/id #uuid "a75c3b50-fff3-5adf-8644-677a0a92aacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollar per 